METADATA:{"created":"2025/09/15 18:52:14","modified":"2025/09/15 22:37:14"}

NOTE BLOCK CONTENT:

(cont'd) ```


Given the word "alignment," your gears must have already started turning - how can we align the three channels so that colored images do not appear distorted? We need some metric that finds the **best overlap between a reference channel and a shifted candidate**:
$$\text{best overlap} = \text{metric}(\text{reference}, \text{target}).$$
A simple choice is a **similarity metric** like **Normalized Cross-Correlation (NCC)** (higher is better) or a **distance metric** like **Sum of Squared Differences (SSD)** (lower is better) between the two images:
$$
\begin{array}{c@{\hspace{10cm}}c}
\text{Sum of Squared Differences (SSD)} & \text{Normalized Cross-Correlation (NCC)} \\[6pt]
\displaystyle \text{SSD}(A,B) = \sum_{i,j} \big(A_{i,j} - B_{i,j}\big)^2 &
\displaystyle \text{NCC}(A,B) = 
\frac{\sum_{i,j} \big(A_{i,j} - \bar{A}\big)\,\big(B_{i,j} - \bar{B}\big)}
     {\sqrt{\sum_{i,j} (A_{i,j} - \bar{A})^2}\;\sqrt{\sum_{i,j} (B_{i,j} - \bar{B})^2}}
\end{array}
$$
For each candidate shift $(\Delta x,\Delta y)$, we compare an inner window of (say) the green channel against the red/blue channel shifted by $(\Delta x,\Delta y)$, then pick the shift that maximizes NCC (or minimizes SSD). However, these two scoring functions do not come without their drawbacks:
- **SSD** ($\ell^2$ norm) is highly sensitive to differences in illumination or exposure, since even small brightness shifts inflate the squared error. An alternative to this could be using the $\ell^1$ norm (SAD), which given the grid-like nature of images could be a contender.
- On the other hand, **NCC**, while more robust to intensity offsets and scaling, is computationally more expensive and can become unstable when the variance in one channel is very small.

A big weakness to consider is that both of these scoring functions are **applied on the pixel brightness** of the images. If the channels aren't normalized or have different exposure levels, then the raw pixel brightness values can mislead the alignment. For example, if one channel is consistently brighter, **SSD** will inflate the error even when edges are well aligned, and **NCC** might become unstable if the variance is very small. This is why many implementations either normalize channels beforehand, or use edge-based features (like Sobel gradients) instead of raw brightness to make the scoring functions more robust. 

I personally chose to use NCC and normalize the channels rather than going for an edge-based approach. Essentially, to normalize each channel $X_{c}$, one must calculate
$$\tilde{X}_c = \frac{X_c - \min(X_c)\,1_{m \times n}}
       {\max(X_c) - \min(X_c)},
\qquad c \in \{B,G,R\}.$$
Limiting the scoring function to a centered window that is $1 / 3$ the size of the image shape reduced the processing time of naive search to around $1 / 4$ of the time for large-resolution files and around $1 / 3$ for smaller files. However, 

