>[!note] Convex Function
>A function $f: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$ is **convex** if its domain $D$ is a [[2.1 Convex Sets|convex set]], and for all points $\vec{x}_{1},\vec{x}_{2}\in D$ and $\alpha \in [0,1]$, the graph of $f$ lies below (or on) the line segment connecting $f(\vec{x}_{1})$ and $f(\vec{x}_{2})$, such that:
>$$f(\alpha \vec{x}_{1}+(1-\alpha)\vec{x}_{2}) \leq \alpha f(\vec{x}_{1})+(1-\alpha)f(\vec{x}_{2}).$$
>If $-f$ is convex, then $f$ is said to be **concave**. If instead the above inequality holds strictly (i.e., with $<$ instead of $\leq$), then $f$ is **strictly convex**. The following is a visual interpretation of convexity:
>```tikz
>\usepackage{pgfplots}
>\begin{document}
>\begin{tikzpicture}
>  \begin{axis}[
>    xlabel={$x$}, xlabel style={align=left},
>    ylabel={$f(x)$},
>    ytick=0,
>    xtick=0,
>    domain=0:6,               % choose a domain that avoids the asymptote at x=1 if desired
>    samples=200,
>    restrict y to domain=0:20,  % only show y-values that are positive (and up to 20)
>    unbounded coords=jump,      % jump over undefined/infinite parts
>    axis lines=center,
>    clip mode=individual]
>    % Plot the function's positive part using max(0, f(x)):
>    \addplot[thick, clip=true] {max(0, x^2/3 + 1/(x-1))};
>    
>    \draw[dashed, thick] (axis cs:5.41,0) -- (axis cs:5.41,10) node[at start, below]{$x_{2}$} node[right]{$f(x_{2})$};
>    \filldraw (axis cs:5.41,10) circle (2pt);
>    \draw[dashed, thick] (axis cs:1.17,0) -- (axis cs:1.17,6.3) node[at start, below]{$x_{1}$} node[left]{$f(x_{1})$};
>    \filldraw (axis cs:1.17,6.3) circle (2pt);
>    \draw (axis cs:1.17,6.3) -- (axis cs:5.41,10) node[midway, above, sloped]{$\alpha f(x_{1})+(1-\alpha)f(x_{2})$};
>    
>    \draw[dotted, thick] (axis cs:3.5,0) -- (axis cs:3.5,8.335) node[at start, below]{$z=\alpha x_1+(1-\alpha)x_2$};
>    \filldraw (axis cs:3.5,4.48) circle (2pt) node[below right]{$f(z)$};
>    \filldraw (axis cs:3.5,8.335) circle (1pt);
>  \end{axis}
>\end{tikzpicture}
>\end{document}
>```
>
>Furthermore, a function $f:\mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$ is strongly convex if there exists some $m$, such that
>$$\tilde{f}(x)=f(x)-\frac{m}{2}\|x\|_{2}^{2}$$
>is convex. Note that strong convexity implies strict convexity, 

# Properties
#### (*Envelope Representation*) If $f$ is convex and l.s.c., $f(x)=\sup\limits_{a \leq f}a(x)$, where $a$ is a collection of affine functions such that $a(x) \leq f(x)$ for all $x \in X$.
You can imagine all of $f$'s tangents "enveloping" $f$: they only intersect one point of $x$, which, in terms of all tangent lines on $f$, constructs $f$. The largest value of any affine $a$ on $x$ will be the point where the affine tangent intersects $f$. We use the supremum instead of the maximum to keep the behavior well-defined for discontinuities.

