NOTE TITLE: [[3.4c Hulls]]

METADATA:{"created":"2025/03/29 20:04:29","modified":"2025/04/16 00:17:20"}

NOTE BLOCK CONTENT:

>[!note] Hull
>A **hull** of a set of vectors $\mathcal{P}=\{ \vec{v}_{1}, \ldots, \vec{v}_{n} \}$ is some finite set generated by all possible linear combinations of $\mathcal{P}$ under certain constraints (i.e., a finite subset of the [[3.4b Span|span]]):
>- An **affine hull** is the set of all linear combinations of $\mathcal{P}$, such that the coefficients $\alpha_{i}$ sum to one:
>$$\operatorname{aff}(\mathcal{P}) = \left\{  \sum^{n}_{i=1}\alpha_{i}\vec{v}_{i} : \space \sum^{n}_{i=1}\alpha_{i}=1 \right\}.$$
>The affine hull is the smallest **affine set** containing $\mathcal{P}$.
>- A **conic hull** is the set of all linear combinations of $\mathcal{P}$, such that the coefficients are non-negative:
>$$\operatorname{conic}(\mathcal{P}) = \left\{  \sum^{n}_{i=1}\alpha_{i}\vec{v}_{i} : \space \alpha_{i} \geq 0 \right\}.$$
>- A **convex hull** is the set of all linear combinations of $\mathcal{P}$, such that the coefficients are constrained by both sum-to-one and non-negativity:
>$$\operatorname{co}(\mathcal{P}) = \left\{  \sum^{n}_{i=1}\alpha_{i}\vec{v}_{i} : \space \sum^{n}_{i=1}\alpha_{i}=1, \alpha_{i} \geq 0 \right\}.$$
>
>It always holds that $\operatorname{co}(\mathcal{P}) \subset \operatorname{aff}(\mathcal{P})$ and $\operatorname{co}(\mathcal{P}) \subset \operatorname{cone}(\mathcal{P})$. Furthermore, if $\vec{0} \in \mathcal{P}$ (i.e., $\mathcal{P}$ is a subspace), then it holds that $\operatorname{co}(\mathcal{P}) \subset \operatorname{cone}(\mathcal{P}) \subset \operatorname{aff}(\mathcal{P})$:
>```tikz
>\begin{document}
>\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
>
>% Define the three points
>\coordinate (A) at (2,2);
>\coordinate (B) at (4,2);
>\coordinate (C) at (3,4);
>
>% Draw coordinate axes
>\draw[->] (-1,0) -- (8,0) node[right] {$x$};
>\draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,8) node[above] {$y$};
>
>% Affine Hull: Since the affine hull of non-collinear points in R^2 is the entire plane,
>% we indicate this by shading the entire drawing area lightly.
>\fill[red, opacity=0.1] (-1,-1) rectangle (8,8);
>\node[red!80!black] at (3.5,7) {co($\mathcal{P}$)};
>
>% Draw and label the three points
>\filldraw (A) circle (2pt) node[below left] {$A(2,2)$};
>\filldraw (B) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$B(4,2)$};
>\filldraw (C) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$C(3,4)$};
>
>% Conic Hull: Compute the extreme angles (in degrees) relative to the origin.
>% For B = (4,2): angle = arctan(2/4) ≈ 26.565°.
>% For C = (3,4): angle = arctan(4/3) ≈ 53.13°.
>\def\angleB{26.565}
>\def\angleC{53.13}
>
>% Draw the extreme rays from the origin for the conic hull.
>
>
>% Fill the conic hull wedge: this fills the region from angleB to angleC from the origin out to radius 10.
>\begin{scope}
>	\clip (-1,-1) rectangle (8,8); % clip to the visible region
>	\draw[thick, green!70!black] (0,0) -- ({12*cos(\angleB)},{12*sin(\angleB)});
>	\draw[thick, green!70!black] (0,0) -- ({12*cos(\angleC)},{12*sin(\angleC)});
>	\fill[green, opacity=0.2] 
>	  (0,0) -- ({12*cos(\angleB)},{12*sin(\angleB)}) 
>	  arc [start angle={\angleB}, end angle={\angleC}, radius=12] -- cycle;
>	\node[green!70!black] at (7,6) {conic($\mathcal{P}$)};
>\end{scope}
>
>% Convex Hull: Draw the triangle (blue)
>\draw[thick, blue] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;
>\fill[blue, opacity=0.25] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;
>\node at (3,2.5) {co($\mathcal{P}$)};
>
>\end{tikzpicture}
>\end{document}
>```
