>[!note] Poisson Process
>A **Poisson process** $N \sim PP(\lambda)$ for some **arrival rate** $\lambda > 0$ is a continuous-time stochastic process $N=\{ N_{t} \}_{t \geq 0}$, such that each $N_{t} \sim \operatorname{Pois}(\lambda t)$ is a [[2.2b.8 Poisson Distribution|Poisson random variable]] that denotes the number of arrivals at time $t$,
>$$N_{t}=\max\{n \geq 0: T_{n} \leq t  \},$$ 
>where $T_{n}=\sum\limits^{n}_{i=1}S_{i} \sim \operatorname{Erlang}(n, \lambda)$ is the cumulative time (i.e., [[2.2c.3 Exponential Distribution|the sum of n Exponential]], independent, and identically distributed arrival times $S_{i} \sim \operatorname{Exp}(\lambda)$) from $t=0$ at which the $n$-th event occurs:
>```tikz
>\usetikzlibrary{decorations.pathreplacing}
>\begin{document}
>
>\large\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1.5, yscale=1]
>  % Axes
>  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (6.5,0) node[right] {$t$} node[at start, below]{$0$};
>  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$N_{t}$};
>
>  % Step function (Poisson process)
>  \draw[very thick, red!70!black]
>    (0,0) -- (0.4,0) node[midway, above, black]{$S_1$}
>    -- (0.4,1) -- (1.6,1) node[midway, above, yshift=-1cm, black]{$S_2$}
>    -- (1.6,2) -- (2.3,2) node[midway, above, yshift=-2cm, black, ]{$S_3$}
>    -- (2.3,3) -- (4,3) node[midway, above, yshift=-3cm, black, ]{$S_4$}
>    -- (4,4) -- (5.3,4) node[midway, above, yshift=-4cm, black, ]{$S_5$}
>    -- (5.3,5) -- (6.5,5);
>
>  % Define lines and plot for each T_i
>  \foreach \x/\y/\label in {0.4/1/$T_1$, 1.6/2/$T_2$, 2.3/3/$T_3$, 4.0/4/$T_4$, 5.3/5/$T_5$} {
>    \draw[dashed] (\x,-0.2) -- (\x,\y) node[at start, below] {\label};
>    \filldraw[red!70!black] (\x,\y) circle (2pt);
>    \draw[dashed] (-0.2,\y) -- (\x,\y);
>  }
>
>  % Define lines for t
>  \draw[dashed] (3.4,0) -- (3.4,5) node[at start, below] {$\tau$};
>  \draw[dashed] (-0.1,3) -- (3.4,3);
>
>  % Arrows and labels for memoryless
>  \draw[->, thick] (3.4,3) -- (3.4,5.5) node[above]{$N_{\tau+u} - N_\tau$} node[at start, below]{$0$};
>  \draw[->, thick] (3.4,3) -- (6.5,3) node[right]{$u$};
>  \node at (4, 2.7) {$U_{1}$};
>  \node at (5.3, 2.7) {$U_{2}$};
>  
>  % Annotation for memoryless
>  \draw [decorate, decoration={brace, amplitude=10pt, raise=-7.5pt}] (3.4,6.5) -- (6.5,6.5) node[midway, above]{Memoryless};
>
>\end{tikzpicture}
>
>\end{document}
>```
>The distribution of each $T_{n} \sim \operatorname{Erlang}(n, \lambda)$ is called the **Erlang distribution**, and represents the distribution of the sum of $n$ independent and identically distributed Exponential variables (in this case, the inter-arrival times) with parameter $\lambda>0$. The probability  
>>[!warning] Memorylessness
>>The Poisson process inherits the [[2.2b.8 Poisson Distribution|memoryless property]] from the Poisson (through the $N_{t}$'s) and Exponential distribution (through the $S_{i}$'s). Note that the Erlang-distributed $T_{n}$'s **are not memoryless**. 
# Rationale
At the core of Poisson Processes are the independent and identically distributed inter-arrival times $S_{i}$. Given that they are Exponentially distributed, we can determine the distribution of every arrival $N_{t}$. We proceed as follows:




# Properties
#### (*Stationary Increments*) $N_{t+\tau}-N_{t} \sim \operatorname{Pois}(\lambda \tau)$.

#### (*Independent Increments*) All $I_{t}=N_{t}-N_{t-1}$ are mutually independent.
#### (*Small Increments*) $\mathbb{P}(N_{t+dt}-N_{t}=k)=\begin{dcases}1-\lambda dt+o(dt) & \text{if } k=0, \\ \lambda dt + o(dt) & \text{if } k=1, \\ o(dt) & \text{if } k \geq 2.\end{dcases}$ 
We know that $I_{t+dt}=N_{t+dt}-N_{t} \sim \operatorname{Pois}(\lambda dt)$. Given this, we have:
$$p_{I_{t+dt}}(k)=e^{-\lambda dt}\frac{(\lambda dt)^k}{k!}=\begin{cases}e^{-\lambda dt} & k=0, \\ \lambda dte^{-\lambda dt} & k=1, \\ \frac{1}{2}(\lambda dt)^2e^{-\lambda dt} & k \geq 2.\end{cases}$$
When $k=0$, 

#### (*Poisson Merging*) Let $N \sim \operatorname{PP}(\lambda)$ and $M \sim \operatorname{PP}(\mu)$ be mutually independent. Then, $N+M \sim \operatorname{PP}(\lambda + \mu)$.
#### (*Poisson Splitting*) Let $N \sim \operatorname{PP}(\lambda)$ and $B_{1},B_{2},\ldots \sim_{\text{iid}} \operatorname{Ber}(p)$ be independent of $N$. Then, $K_{t}=|\{i : B_{i}=0, i \leq N_{t}\}|$ and $J_{t}=|\{ i : B_{i}=1,i \leq N_{t}\}|$ such that $K \sim \operatorname{PP}(\lambda p)$, and $J \sim \operatorname{PP}(\lambda[1-p])$ are mutually independent.
In poisson splitting, each arrival to $N_{t}$ is routed to $K_{t}$ or $J_{t}$ according to independent Bernoulli trials, where $B_{i}$ determines the probability of the $i$-th arrival going to $K_t$ or $J_{t}$.

