467.content:
NOTE TITLE: [[6.1c Cash Flow Statement]]

METADATA:{"created":"2025/03/16 22:22:21","modified":"2025/03/17 12:43:35"}

NOTE BLOCK CONTENT:

>[!note] Statement of Cash Flows
>The **statement of cash flows** is a financial report that tracks the movement of cash in and out of a company over a specific period. It categorizes cash flows into three main activities, which it equates to net income, such that
>$$\text{Net Income} = \text{Operating Cash Flow} + \text{Investment Cash Flow} + \text{Financing Cash Flow}.$$
>The following example shows the trickle-down equation from the starting to the ending cash balance:
>```tikz
>\usepackage{tikz}
>\usepackage{amsmath}
>
>\begin{document}
>
>\begin{tikzpicture}
>    % Draw table borders
>    \draw (0,-1.55) -- (12,-1.55);
>    \draw (0,-5.55) -- (12,-5.55);
>    \draw (0,-5.65) -- (12,-5.65);
>
>
>
>    % Left column text
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,0.2) {\textbf{BEGINNING CASH BALANCE}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-0.8) {\textbf{CASH RECEIPTS}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-1.3) {\textbf{CASH DISBURSEMENTS}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-1.8) {\textbf{CASH FROM OPERATIONS}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-2.8) {\textbf{CAPITAL EXPENDITURES}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-3.3) {\textbf{NET BORROWINGS}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-3.8) {\textbf{INCOME TAXES}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-4.3) {\textbf{SALE OF STOCK}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-5.3) {\textbf{ENDING CASH BALANCE}};
>
>    % Right column values
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,0.2) {A};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-0.8) {B};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-1.3) {C};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-1.8) {B - C = D};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-2.8) {E};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-3.3) {F};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-3.8) {G};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-4.3) {H};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-5.3) {A + D - E + F - G + H = I};
>\end{tikzpicture}
>
>\end{document}
>```
>The statement of cash flows serves as a bridge between the income statement and the balance sheet by tracking the movement of cash within a company. It adjusts net income from the income statement and explains changes in balance sheet accounts to show how much cash was generated or used.
>
>>[!warning] Accrual vs. Cash Accounting
>>Whereas the balance sheet and the income statement are accrual (they recognize revenue and expenses when incurred), the statement of cash flows reports transactions **when cash actually moves**.

```tikz
\usepackage{tikz}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\begin{document}

\begin{tikzpicture}
    % Draw table borders
    \draw (0,-1.55) -- (12,-1.55);
    \draw (0,-5.55) -- (12,-5.55);
    \draw (0,-5.65) -- (12,-5.65);



    % Left column text
    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,0.2) {\textbf{BEGINNING CASH BALANCE}};
    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-0.8) {\textbf{CASH RECEIPTS}};
    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-1.3) {\textbf{CASH DISBURSEMENTS}};
    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-1.8) {\textbf{CASH FROM OPERATIONS}};
    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-2.8) {\textbf{CAPITAL EXPENDITURES}};
    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-3.3) {\textbf{NET BORROWINGS}};
    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-3.8) {\textbf{INCOME TAXES}};
    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-4.3) {\textbf{SALE OF STOCK}};
    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-5.3) {\textbf{ENDING CASH BALANCE}};

    % Right column values
    \node[anchor=west] at (7,0.2) {A};
    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-0.8) {B};
    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-1.3) {C};
    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-1.8) {B - C = D};
    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-2.8) {E};
    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-3.3) {F};
    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-3.8) {G};
    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-4.3) {H};
    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-5.3) {A + D - E + F - G + H = I};
\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}
```

468.content:
NOTE TITLE: [[6.1b Income Statement]]

METADATA:{"created":"2025/03/16 18:52:52","modified":"2025/03/17 12:41:48"}

NOTE BLOCK CONTENT:

>[!note] Income Statement
>The **income statement** is a financial report that summarizes a company’s revenues, expenses, and net profit over a specific period. It helps assess a company’s profitability and operational efficiency. In essence, a company's income statement must equal the formula 
>$$\text{Net Income} = \text{Revenue} - \text{Expenses}.$$
>The following example shows the trickle-down equation from revenue to net income:
>```tikz
>\usepackage{tikz}
>\usepackage{amsmath}
>
>\begin{document}
>
>\begin{tikzpicture}
>    % Draw table borders
>    \draw (0,-0.55) -- (10,-0.55);
>    \draw (0,-3.05) -- (10,-3.05);
>    \draw (0,-7.05) -- (10,-7.05);
>    \draw (0,-7.15) -- (10,-7.15);
>
>
>    % Left column text
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,0.2) {\textbf{NET SALES}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-0.3) {\textbf{COST OF GOODS SOLD}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-0.8) {\textbf{GROSS INCOME}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-1.8) {\textbf{SALES \& MARKETING}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-2.3) {\textbf{RESEARCH \& DEVELOPMENT}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-2.8) {\textbf{GENERAL \& ADMINISTRATIVE}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-3.3) {\textbf{OPERATING EXPENSES}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-4.3) {\textbf{INCOME FROM OPERATIONS}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-5.3) {\textbf{INTEREST INCOME}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-5.8) {\textbf{INCOME TAXES}};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (0.2,-6.8) {\textbf{NET INCOME}};
>
>    % Right column values
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,0.2) {A};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-0.2) {B};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-0.8) {A - B = C};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-1.8) {D};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-2.3) {E};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-2.8) {F};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-3.3) {D + E + F = G};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-4.3) {C - G = H};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-5.3) {I};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-5.8) {J};
>    \node[anchor=west] at (7,-6.8) {H + I - J = K};
>\end{tikzpicture}
>
>\end{document}
>```
>The bottom line (i.e., net income) of the income statement flows into the [[6.1a Balance Sheet|balance sheet]] under shareholders’ equity.
>
>>[!warning] Revenue $\neq$ Sales
>>Revenue is total income generated from all business activities, including sales.

502.content:
NOTE TITLE: [[2.1a.2 Yield Curves]]

METADATA:{"created":"2025/02/25 17:47:56","modified":"2025/03/29 14:18:44"}

NOTE BLOCK CONTENT:

>[!note] Yield Curves
>[[2.1a.1 Bonds|Bond yields]] can be plotted against their respective maturities to create a **yield curve**, which graphically represents a snapshot of the term structure of interest rates at a given point in time. The difference between yield curves is known as the **credit spread**, and quantifies the risk of a bond given a risk-free benchmark:
>
>```tikz
>\usepackage{tikz}
>\usepackage{amsmath}
>\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta, decorations.pathreplacing}
>
>\begin{document}
>
>\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9, >=stealth]
>
>  % Axes
>  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (6,0) node[right] {Maturity (Years)};
>  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5) node[above] {Yield (bp)};
>  
>  \draw[thick] (0.5,0.8) arc (150:105:8cm and 8cm) node[right]{Corporate Bond};
>  \draw[thick] (0.5,0.4) arc (140:106:10cm and 6cm) node[right]{Treasury Bond};
>  
>  % Credit Spread
>  \draw[dashed] (3.4, 0) -- (3.4, 5) node[at start, below] {$t$};
>  \draw[thick, <->, color=purple] (3.4, 1.8) -- (3.4, 3.7) node[midway, right, yshift=0.25cm] {Credit Spread};
>  
>  % -- X-axis ticks & labels for maturities
>  \foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5}
>    \draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node[below] {\x};
>
>  % -- Y-axis ticks & labels (just an example in increments of 1)
>  %    Interpreting each 1 as 20 bp (or your chosen scale).
>  \foreach \y in {1,2,3,4}
>    \draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y)
>          node[left] {\pgfmathparse{20*\y}\pgfmathresult};
>
>\end{tikzpicture}
>
>\end{document}
>```
>
>The yield curve of stripped (zero-coupon) bonds is known as the **spot rate** curve, and can be derived via the process of **bootstrapping** from the yield curve. Spot rates, which represent the zero-coupon yield at a certain snapshot of time, are crucial in determining **forward rates** (i.e., the rate which makes an investor indifferent between two or more bonds), such that the $\tau$-period forward rate starting at time $t$ is
>$$f_{t,t+\tau}=\left[\frac{(1+S_{t+\tau})^{t + \tau}}{(1+S_{t})^\tau}\right]^{1/\tau}-1,$$
>for some spot rates $S_{i}$.

