METADATA:{"created":"2025/03/08 00:09:55","modified":"2025/03/09 04:06:38"}

NOTE BLOCK CONTENT:

>[!note] Singular Value Decomposition
>A general matrix $A \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}$ with rank $r = \operatorname{Rank}(A)$  can be decomposed as
>$$A = U\Upsigma V^T$$
>where $U = \begin{bmatrix}U_r & U_{m - r}\end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times m}$ and $V_r = \begin{bmatrix}V_r & V_{n - r}\end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ are square orthonormal matrices whose columns are known respectively as the **left singular vectors** and **right singular vectors** of $A$, whereas $\Upsigma = \begin{bmatrix}\Upsigma_r & 0_{r \times (n-r)} \\ 0_{(m-r) \times r} & 0_{(m-r) \times (n-r)}\end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}$ is a block matrix with $\Sigma_{r} = \operatorname{diag}(\sigma_{1}, \ldots, \sigma_{r}) \succ 0$ having positive ordered diagonal entries known as the **singular values** of $A$, such that for $AA^T$ and $A^TA$, $\sigma_{i} = \sqrt{ \lambda_{i} }$.
>
>The SVD of $A$ can also be rewritten in **compact-form**, such that
>$$A=U_{r}\Sigma_{r}V_{r}^T = \sum^{r}_{i=1}\sigma_{i}\vec{u}_{i}\vec{v}_{i}^T,$$
>where $A\vec{v}_{i}=\sigma_{i}\vec{v}_{i}$ and $\vec{u}^TA=\sigma_{i}\vec{u}^T_{i}$.
>
>

# Intuition
We can geometrically interpret SVD by considering what each component of the decomposition does when considering $A$ as a linear transformation. Take a look at this illustration:
```tikz
