NOTE TITLE: [[2.10a Sampling]]

METADATA:{"created":"2024/12/17 04:34:08","modified":"2024/12/17 15:30:36"}

NOTE BLOCK CONTENT:

(cont'd) # Intuition


# Examples
>[!example] The Carriage Wheel Effect
>Have you ever seen the wheels move the opposite way in a car commercial? That is called the carriage wheel effect. Suppose a wheel can be modeled by $x(t)=e^{j\omega_{0} t}$; intuitively, this makes sense, since as $t \to \infty$, $x(t)$ moves periodically anti-clockwise. Then, using CTFT, $X(\omega) = 2\pi\delta(\omega-\omega_{0})$. We sample $x(t)$ at $\omega_{S}=\frac{2\pi}{T_{S}}$. 
>
>Suppose we choose the sampling frequency $\omega_{S}=\omega_{0}$. Then, $x[n]$ appears to be stationary. Intuitively, if $\omega_{S}$ is the frame rate (frames per second), and $\omega_{0}$ measures the spin of the wheels of the car, then each frame is recorded exactly at the same spot of the wheel. We can verify this by taking the CTFT of the impulse train, $Z(\omega)=\omega_{S}\sum^{\infty}_{k=-\infty}\delta\left( \omega - k\omega_{S} \right)$, and performing the modulation
>$$\begin{align}
>x(t) \times z(t)&=\frac{1}{2\pi}(X * Z)(\omega) \\
>&= \omega_{S}\int^{\infty}_{-\infty}\sum^{\infty}_{k=-\infty}\delta\left( \omega - k\omega_{S} \right)\delta(\omega-\omega_{0})d\omega  \\
>&= \omega_{S}\sum^{\infty}_{k=-\infty}\delta\left( \omega - k\omega_{S} - \omega_{0} \right) \\
&= \omega_{S}\sum^{\infty}_{k=-\infty}\delta\left( \omega - [k\omega_{S} + \omega_{0}] \right),
\end{align}$$
>by the sifting property of the Dirac delta. Then, $X_{Z}(\omega)$ can be graphically represented as:
>```tikz
>\usepackage{tikz}
>\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
>
>\begin{document}
>\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2, thick]
>
>% Axes
>\draw[->] (-4, 0) -- (6, 0) node[right] {$\omega$};
>\draw[->] (0, -0.5) -- (0, 2.5) node[above] {$X_z(\omega)$};
>\draw[-] (-0.125, 1.5) -- (0.125, 1.5) node[at start, left]{$\omega_S$};
>
>% Lines for arrows
>\draw[->] (1, 0) -- (1, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$\omega_0$};
>\draw[->] (5, 0) -- (5, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$\omega_0+\omega_S$};
>\draw[->] (-3, 0) -- (-3, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$\omega_0-\omega_S$};
>
>\end{tikzpicture}
>\end{document}
>```
>
>For $\omega_{S}=\omega_{0}$, this means that only one of the impulses will be preserved after reconstructing the signal:
>```tikz
>\usepackage{tikz}
>\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
>
>\begin{document}
>\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2, thick]
>
>% Axes
>\draw[->] (-4, 0) -- (6, 0) node[right] {$\omega$};
>\draw[->] (0, -0.5) -- (0, 2.5) node[above] {$X_z(\omega)$};
>\draw[-] (-0.125, 1.5) -- (0.125, 1.5) node[at start, left]{$\omega_S$};
>
>% Lines for arrows
>\draw[->, red] (0, 0) -- (0, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$\omega_0 - \omega_{0}$};
>\draw[->] (3, 0) -- (3, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$\omega_0$};
>\draw[->] (-3, 0) -- (-3, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$-\omega_0$};
>
>% Draw low pass
>\draw[dashed] (1.5, 0) node[below]{$\frac{\omega_0}{2}$} -- (1.5, 2) -- (-1.5, 2) -- (-1.5, 0) node[below]{$-\frac{\omega_0}{2}$};
>
>\end{tikzpicture}
>\end{document}
>```
>Therefore, we perceive the wheels as stationary, as $x(t)=e^{j 0 t}=1 \text{ for all } t.$
>
>For $\omega_{S}=\frac{3\omega_{0}}{2}$, we get the following graph:
>```tikz
>\usepackage{tikz}
>\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
>
>\begin{document}
>\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2, thick]
>
>% Axes
>\draw[->] (-4, 0) -- (6, 0) node[right] {$\omega$};
>\draw[->] (0, -0.5) -- (0, 2.5) node[above] {$X_z(\omega)$};
>\draw[-] (-0.125, 1.5) -- (0.125, 1.5) node[at start, left]{$\omega_S$};
>
>% Lines for arrows
>\draw[->] (2, 0) -- (2, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$\omega_{0}$};
>\draw[->] (5, 0) -- (5, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$\frac{5 \omega_{0}}{2}$};
>\draw[->, red] (-1, 0) -- (-1, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$-\frac{\omega_{0}}{2}$};
>\draw[->] (-4, 0) -- (-4, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$-2\omega_{0}$};
>
>% Draw low pass
>\draw[dashed] (1.5, 0) node[below]{$\frac{3\omega_0}{4}$} -- (1.5, 2) -- (-1.5, 2) -- (-1.5, 0) node[below]{$-\frac{3\omega_0}{4}$};
>
>\end{tikzpicture}
>\end{document}
>```
>which makes $x(t)=e^{-j \frac{\omega_{0}}{2}t}$ appear to go clockwise. Therefore, the recording shows wheels spinning in the opposite direction of movement.
>
>Finally, for $\omega_{S}=\frac{5}{2}\omega_{0}$, we get:
>```tikz
>\usepackage{tikz}
>\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
>
>\begin{document}
>\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2, thick]
>
>% Axes
>\draw[->] (-4, 0) -- (6, 0) node[right] {$\omega$};
>\draw[->] (0, -0.5) -- (0, 2.5) node[above] {$X_z(\omega)$};
>\draw[-] (-0.125, 1.5) -- (0.125, 1.5) node[at start, left]{$\omega_S$};
>
>% Lines for arrows
>\draw[->, red] (1.2, 0) -- (1.2, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$\omega_{0}$};
>\draw[->] (4.2, 0) -- (4.2, 1.5) node[at start, below]{$\frac{7}{2}\omega_{0}$};
>\draw[->] (-1.8, 0) -- (-1.8, 1.5) node[at start, above left]{$-\frac{3}{2}\omega_{0}$};
>
>% Draw low pass
>\draw[dashed] (1.5, 0) node[below]{$\frac{5}{4}\omega_0$} -- (1.5, 2) -- (-1.5, 2) -- (-1.5, 0) node[below]{$-\frac{5}{4}\omega_0$};
>
>\end{tikzpicture}
>\end{document}
>```
>These 3 cases show us that the camera correctly captures the spin of the wheels, only when
>$$\omega_{S} \geq 2 \omega_{0}.$$

