NOTE TITLE: [[2.3d Collective Action Failures]]

METADATA:{"created":"2024/06/30 07:44:09","modified":"2024/06/30 14:18:17"}

NOTE BLOCK CONTENT:

>[!note] Collective Action Failure
>A collective action failure occurs when a collective action game's [[2.2c Nash Equilibrium|Nash equilibrium]] is not [[2.3b Pareto Efficiency|Pareto efficient]]. Collective action failures are corrected by a persuasive authority which removes the inconsistency.

# Examples
>[!example] Prisoner's Dilemma
>Consider a classic game of the Prisoner's Dilemma, where each prisoner can either cooperate or defect. Their payoffs represent the amount of utility they get from their sentencing, such that the smaller the sentence, the happier the prisoner:
>```tikz
>\begin{document}
>
>\begin{tikzpicture}
>    % Draw the vertical lines
>    \draw (0, 0) -- (0, -3);
>    \draw (1.5, 0) -- (1.5, -3);
>    \draw (3, 0) -- (3, -3);
>
>    % Draw the horizontal lines
>    \draw (0, 0) -- (3, 0);
>    \draw (0, -1.5) -- (3, -1.5);
>    \draw (0, -3) -- (3, -3);
>
>    % Add labels for players
>    \node at (1.5, 1) {Prisoner 2};
>    \node[rotate=90] at (-1, -1.5) {Prisoner 1};
>
>    % Add labels for Player 2's choices
>    \node at (0.75, 0.25) {C};
>    \node at (2.25, 0.25) {D};
>
>    % Add labels for Player 1's choices
>    \node at (-0.25, -0.75) {C};
>    \node at (-0.25, -2.25) {D};
>
>    % Add payoffs for each cell
>    \node at (0.75, -0.75) {(2, 2)};
>    \node at (2.25, -0.75) {(0, 3)};
>
>    \node at (0.75, -2.25) {(3, 0)};
>    \node at (2.25, -2.25) {(1, 1)};
>\end{tikzpicture}
>
>\end{document}
>```
>Through a best response analysis, we see that the Nash equilibrium is at $(D, D)$. However, this is inconsistent with the collective good of the prisoners. As such, coercive authority is granted to a state actor, forcing the prisoners to act in a Pareto efficient manner and choosing $(C, C)$. This is known as the *liberal justification* for a coercive state, where people freely enact an authority to guarantee that they behave in a certain way.

NOTE TITLE: [[2.4b Feedback]]

METADATA:{"created":"2024/04/08 16:03:51","modified":"2024/12/15 03:59:53"}

NOTE BLOCK CONTENT:

>[!note]  Feedback (Black's Formula)
>**Feedback** refers to the process in which a portion (or all) of the output of a system is returned (fed back) to its input, such a system $H$ in  **positive feedback** has the following block diagram:
>```tikz
>\usepackage{amsmath}
>\usetikzlibrary{positioning}
>
>\begin{document}
>\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.3, transform shape]
>  % Main Node
>  \node (s) {\(P(\omega)\)};
>  \draw[thick] (1, -0.5) -- (-1, -0.5) -- (-1, 0.5) -- (1, 0.5) -- cycle;
>
>  % Feedback Node
>  \node[below=of s] (in) {\(K(\omega)\)};
>  \draw[thick] (0.75, -2.15) -- (-0.75, -2.15) -- (-0.75, -1.15) -- (0.75, -1.15) -- cycle;
>
>  % Arrows
>  \draw[->, thick] (-4, 0) -- (-2.2, 0) node[at start, left]{$x(t)$};
>  \draw[->, thick] (1, 0) -- (4, 0) node[at end, right]{$y(t)$};
>  \draw[->, thick] (2, 0) -- (2, -1.65) |- (0.75, -1.65);
>  \draw[->, thick] (-0.75, -1.65) -- (-2, -1.65) |- (-2, -0.2);
>  
>  \draw[thick, dashed] (-2.5, 1) -- (2.5, 1) -- (2.5, -2.5) -- (-2.5, -2.5) -- cycle;
>  \node at (-2.25, -2.25) {H} ;
>  
>  \draw[thick] (-2,0) circle[radius=0.175] node{+};
>  \draw[->, thick] (-1.825, 0) -- (-1.05, 0);
>\end{tikzpicture}
>\end{document}
>```
>where $P(\omega)$ is the system being controlled (known as the **plant**), and $K(\omega)$ is the device that adjusts the plant’s input based on the difference between desired and actual output, also known as the **controller**.
>
>Harold Black's solution to a feedback system was to instead apply **negative feedback** (i.e. when the adder on the left is negative) to a system. The transfer function of such a system can be expressed as the forward gain divided by 1 plus the loop gain, such that
>$$H(\omega) = \frac{P(\omega)}{1+K(\omega)P(\omega)} \approx \frac{1}{K(\omega)} \enspace \text{(for large loop gains)}.$$
>Black argued that since positive feedback is unstable, negative feedback is a more stable alternative to traditional (positive) feedback.
# Proof
We proceed by analyzing the block diagram for continuous-time LTI systems. If the input to the feedback system $K$ is $y(t) = H(\omega)e^{j\omega t}$, then the input to the system $P$ (since convolution is multiplication in the frequency domain) is
$$\begin{align}
z(t) &= e^{j\omega t} + H(\omega)K(\omega)e^{j\omega t} \\
&= [1+H(\omega)K(\omega)]e^{j\omega t}
\end{align}$$
Its output then becomes
$$y(t)=[1+H(\omega)K(\omega)]P(\omega)e^{j\omega t}.$$
However, since $y(t) = H(\omega)e^{j \omega t}$,
$$H(\omega)=\frac{P(\omega)}{1-K(\omega)P(\omega)}$$
Since in Black's solution, the adder is negative (the system is in negative feedback), this means that the loop gain is also negative. As such,
$$H(\omega) = \frac{P(\omega)}{1 + K(\omega)P(\omega)}.$$
# Examples
>[!example] Stabilizing an Unstable System
>Consider a current source in series with a capacitor. Such a system has the equation
>$$i_{s}(t) = C\frac{d}{dt}v_{1}(t) \iff \frac{d}{dt}v_{1}(t) = \frac{1}{C}i_{s}(t).$$
>Let's consider the impulse response of this system. For $i_{s}(t)=\delta(t)$,
>$$v_{1}(t) = h(t) = \frac{1}{C}u(t),$$
>since by definition $\int\delta(\tau)d\tau = u(t)$. However, this system is not BIBO stable; $\int^{\infty}_{-\infty}|h(\tau)|d\tau = \infty$. We can fix this by adding a resistor in parallel with the capacitor. Here, the voltage $v_{1}(t)$ is the same, but the current $i_{s}(t)$ is divided across the resistor and the capacitor. The differential equation for this new circuit is
>$$i_{s}(t) = C\frac{d}{dt}v_{1}(t) + \frac{1}{R}v_{1}(t) \iff \frac{d}{dt}v_{1}(t) = \frac{1}{C}i_{s}(t) - \frac{1}{RC}v_{1}(t).$$
>Here, we essentially have a resistor in negative feedback, as seen in the differential equation above. The impulse response to $i_{s}(t) = \delta(t)$ of this circuit is
>$$h(t) = \frac{1}{C}e^{-\frac{1}{RC}t}u(t),$$
>which is a standard solution to a first-order differential equation. This RC circuit is now BIBO stable, with a bound $\int^{\infty}_{-\infty}|h(\tau)|d\tau = R$ (sanity-check this on your own).

NOTE TITLE: [[2.5a D-T Internal Stability]]

METADATA:{"created":"2024/03/31 00:01:37","modified":"2024/12/13 02:16:46"}

NOTE BLOCK CONTENT:

>[!note] Stability in Discrete-Time LTI Difference Equation Model w/ No Input
>For an **input-less** [[2.1a.1 Discrete-Time LTI System|discrete-time LTI model]] $\vec{x}[i+1]=A\vec{x}[i]$ with $\vec{x}[0]=\vec{x}_0$, where $A \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ has eigenvalues $\lambda_1,...,\lambda_n$, the model is:
>1. **Internally stable**, if $|\lambda_i|$ < 1 for all $i$.
>2. **Marginally stable**, if
>	- The absolute largest value among all eigenvalues is exactly 1.
>	- Any eigenvalue that has a magnitude of 1 is unique.
>
>Discrete-time stability can be illustrated on the Cartesian plane as
>```tikz
>\usepackage{tikz}
>\usepackage{pgfplots}
>\begin{document}
>\begin{tikzpicture}
>	% draw the coordinates
>	\draw[->] (-2.5, 0) -- (2.5, 0) node[right] {$Re\{\lambda\}$};
>	\draw[->] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$Im\{\lambda\}$};
>	\pgfsetfillopacity{0.25}
>	% draw the unit circle
>	\draw[thick, color=yellow, fill=red!30] (0, 0) circle(1.5);
>\end{tikzpicture}
>\end{document}
>```
>Where the light-red-filled area of the circle ($|\lambda|<1$) is where internal stability is guaranteed, and the circle's yellow perimeter ($|\lambda|=1$) is where marginal stability may occur. Non-colored areas ($|\lambda|>1$) indicate where internal stability is guaranteed *not* to occur.

