> [!note] Matrix
> A **matrix** $A \in \mathbb{R}^{r \times c}$ is a rectangular arrangement of elements into $r$ rows and $c$ columns, written in the form
> $$A=
>\begin{bmatrix}  
>\vert & \vert &  & \vert \\
>\vec{a}_1 & \vec{a}_2 & \cdots & \vec{a}_c \\ 
>\vert & \vert &  & \vert \\
>\end{bmatrix}
> =
> \begin{bmatrix}  
> A_{11} & A_{12} & \dots & A_{1c} \\  
> A_{21} & A_{22} & ... & A_{2c} \\
> \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\  
> A_{r1} & A_{r2} & ... & A_{rc}
>\end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{r \times c},$$
>where each element $A_{r_ic_i}$ represents the element located at column index $c_i$, and row index $r_i$. The transpose of $A$, denoted $A^T$, is obtained by flipping the matrix along its diagonal, such that
>$$A^T=
>\begin{bmatrix}  
>\enspace — & \vec{a}_1^T & — \enspace \\  
\enspace — & \vec{a}_2^T & — \enspace \\
>& \vdots & \\  
\enspace — & \vec{a}_c^T & — \enspace
>\end{bmatrix}
>=
>\begin{bmatrix}
>A_{11} & A_{21} & \dots & A_{r1} \\
>A_{12} & A_{22} & \dots & A_{r2} \\
>\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
>A_{1r} & A_{2r}& \dots & A_{rc}
>\end{bmatrix}
>\in \mathbb{R}^{c \times r},
>$$
> meaning that $A$'s rows become $A^T$'s columns and vice-versa.


